# M1_limitation_pileup_ML.ode -- where BRUTE FORCE (method M1) fails: the pile-up at the equilibrium,
#                Morris-Lecar oscillator
#
# ======================================================================================
# WHAT TO DO IN THE XPPAUT WINDOW   (or run M1_limitation_pileup_ML.xps: File > Do script)
# ======================================================================================
#  This is M1_ML.ode with the window shrunk to a small box around the equilibrium
#  x* = (-23.09182, 0.1580528) inside the cycle: 2 wide in v, 0.02 in w.
#   1. Viewaxes > 2D: X-axis vs, Y-axis ws, Xlabel v, Ylabel w               (keys v 2)
#   2. nUmerics > Color code > Another quantity: theta, Choose, Min 0, Max 1   (keys u c a)
#   3. Initialconds > 2par range                                              (keys i 2)
#        Vary1 vs  Start1 -24.09182  End1 -22.09182     Vary2 ws  Start2 0.1480528  End2 0.1680528
#        Steps 40  Steps2 40  Reset storage N  Use old ic's Y  Crv(1) Array(2) 2
#      -> every colour (every phase from 0 to 1) appears in this tiny box, in thin
#         interleaved bands.  Points at x* itself never spike: theta = -1 (no phase).
#   4. Nullcline > New (keys n n): both nullclines cross at x*, in the middle of the box.
#   5. Makewindow > Create (keys m c); in the new window repeat step 2 (a new window
#      copies the colour settings but not the quantity), then a box 10 times smaller
#      around x* (Viewaxes and 2par range with Start/End closer to x*): still every colour.  However fine the
#      grid, the box around x* holds all the phases, so the isochrons there cannot be
#      drawn from a grid.  The isochrons all end at x* (a "phaseless point"), and they
#      pile up along the slow part of the cycle, where the colours change faster than
#      any grid.
#
# WHY M1 FAILS HERE:  M1 samples the phase on a grid and reads isochrons off it.  Near
#  x* (and along the thin layer where the isochrons pile up) the phase changes by a whole
#  period over distances far smaller than any practical grid spacing: the grid aliases.
#  The isochron through a point there is still well defined; M1 just cannot resolve it.
#  M3 (continuation) follows each isochron as a curve, so it does not have this problem.
#
# ======================================================================================
# THE METHOD (M1, brute force)
# ======================================================================================
#  Ingredients (computed once, numbers below):  the limit cycle Γ, its period T, a phase
#  zero gamma0 on Γ (the point of Γ with the largest v), and a spike threshold thr.
#
#  For every starting point x0 on a grid:
#   step 1  integrate x0 for a time trans, long enough to settle onto Γ
#           (Γ attracts by about 10^4 per period)
#   step 2  record s = the time of an upward crossing of v = thr after settling
#   step 3  the same crossing for the cycle itself: starting at gamma0, Γ first crosses
#           thr upward at t = tc0.  x0 is then late by (s - tc0) compared with gamma0,
#           so its ASYMPTOTIC PHASE (clock reading) is
#                theta(x0) = (tc0 - s)/T   mod 1
#   step 4  the isochron of phase th = the set of grid points with theta = th:
#           the colour bands (window 1), or the points within dth of th (window 2)
#
#  Why it works: x0 and the point of Γ with phase theta(x0) approach each other, so they
#  cross thr at the same times once x0 has settled.  Only forward integration is needed.
#  What it costs: one full integration per grid point; the grid sets the resolution.
#  Its limitation: see M1_limitation_pileup_ML.ode (all phases crowd into the
#  equilibrium x* = (-23.09182, 0.1580528) inside Γ, which no grid resolves).
#
#  How this file is laid out (XPP draws nullclines only when the plot axes are variables):
#   (vs, ws)  the starting point, as variables that barely move (their equations are
#              eps times the model's, eps = 1e-9): 2par range varies their initial values,
#              window 1 plots them, and their nullclines are the model's nullclines
#   (vb, wb)  the same again, for window 2; a point whose phase is not near k/niso is
#              moved down off the picture at its first spike after settling
#   (V, W)    the trajectory itself, started from (vs, ws) at t = 1e-6
# ======================================================================================

# --- the model: Morris-Lecar (Rinzel & Ermentrout's parameters, i = 100: oscillating)
minf(x)=.5*(1+tanh((x-va1)/va2))
winf(x)=.5*(1+tanh((x-va3)/va4))
tauw(x)=1/cosh((x-va3)/(2*va4))
fx(x,y)=(i-gl*(x-vl)-gca*minf(x)*(x-vca)-gk*y*(x-vk))/c
fy(x,y)=phi*(winf(x)-y)/tauw(x)
par i=100,c=20,gl=2,vl=-60,gca=4.4,vca=120,gk=8,vk=-84
par va1=-1.2,va2=18,va3=2,va4=30,phi=0.04

# --- the ingredients: Γ has period per; it crosses v = thr upward for the first time
#     tc0 after gamma0 = (33.32581, 0.3054107), the point of Γ with the largest v
par per=85.29064, thr=-8.505133, tc0=77.87963

# --- step 1: the starting point x0 = (vs, ws) (and its copy (vb, wb) for window 2)
par eps=1e-9
vs'=eps*fx(vs,ws)
ws'=eps*fy(vs,ws)
vb'=eps*fx(vb,wb)
wb'=eps*fy(vb,wb)
init vs=-23.09182,ws=0.1580528,vb=-23.09182,wb=0.1580528
#     the trajectory from x0: it starts at x0 at t = 1e-6
V'=fx(V,W)
W'=fy(V,W)
init V=-23.09182,W=0.1580528
global 1 t-1e-6 {V=vs;W=ws;vb=vs;wb=ws}

# --- step 2 and step 3: at every upward crossing of v = thr (time t = s), the asymptotic
#     phase theta = (tc0 - s)/T mod 1 (theta stays -1 until the first spike, e.g. for a
#     start at the equilibrium).  Window 2: at a crossing after settling (t > tset), a
#     point whose phase is farther than dth from every multiple of 1/niso is moved down
#     off the picture.
par niso=10, dth=0.012, tset=238.814
theta'=0
init theta=-1
global 1 V-thr {theta=mod((tc0-t)/per,1);wb=if((t>tset)*(abs(mod(mod((tc0-t)/per,1)*niso+0.5,1)-0.5)>(dth*niso)))then(wb-10)else(wb)}

# --- numerics: settle for 4 periods, then store the last 1.3 periods (every 50th step)
@ meth=rk4, dt=0.1, trans=341.163, total=452.04, nout=50, bound=1e6, maxstor=1000000
#     every run of a range starts again from its own point ("Use old ic's" = Y, XPP's
#     default; with N each run would go on from where the last one ended)
@ rangereset=no, rangeoldic=yes
@ xp=vs, yp=ws, xlo=-24.09182, xhi=-22.09182, ylo=0.1480528, yhi=0.1680528, lt=-3, colormap=1, nmesh=200
done
